# bitcoinNetworks Code Reading Notes

The entry file is `frontend/src/app/docs/api-docs/api-docs-data.ts`. First look at the declarations at the top of the file:

```ts
const bitcoinNetworks = ["", "testnet", "signet"];
const liquidNetworks = ["liquid", "liquidtestnet"];
const miningTimeIntervals = "<code>24h</code>, <code>3d</code>, <code>1w</code>, <code>1m</code>, <code>3m</code>, <code>6m</code>, <code>1y</code>, <code>2y</code>, <code>3y</code>";
```

Starting at line 5 is `emptyCodeSample`. The file header also lists four direct dependencies: `_thread`, `time`, `rel`, and `json`. The declaration names on lines 12 and 13 are `showJsExamplesDefault` and `showJsExamplesDefaultFalse`, respectively; line 15 is `wsApiDocsData`. At this point, only these names and their relative positions are confirmed; continue tracing from the caller along `wsApiDocsData`.

# Unix Socket Fallback History

Leave a record of the two issues before this round was closed out. One is near the Esplora request entry point: when the local Unix socket refuses the connection, should the query return the error directly, or can it retain the original request target and try once with an ordinary HTTP? The other is mined spend-map conflict. The two appeared in the same round of discussion, but there is currently no reason to treat them as the same failure chain.

The investigation started from `backend/src/api/bitcoin/esplora-api.ts`. What can be confirmed so far is that the file header references configuration, the HTTP client, Node's http capabilities, and the abstract Bitcoin API:

```ts
import config from '../../config';
import axios, { AxiosRequestConfig } from 'axios';
import http from 'http';
import { AbstractBitcoinApi } from './bitcoin-api-abstract-factory';
```

This indicates that the request options may be assembled near this entry point, and also that the socket is not an isolated string configuration. It does not show the relationships among all callers, much less allow us to infer from the directory name which layer is responsible for recovery. For now, treat this only as the starting point for tracing back.

Three handling directions were listed at the time:

- Fail directly if the socket cannot connect, leaving it to the caller to decide whether to switch entry points;
- Leave the request URL unchanged, remove only socketPath, and try again;
- Switch to another service address.

The third option was set aside early. If the address is changed to work around the local connection problem, the returned data may look normal, but it would be difficult to tell whether it still came from the service target of the original request. The first option is the simplest, but one local connection refusal would leave the mempool query with no fallback path. The tentative preference is the second option, but there is not enough material yet to determine which errors it should cover.

The key issue here is not "whether there is a second request," but what the second request changes. URL represents the request target, while socketPath is the local transport selection; if both change, the result cannot be interpreted as recovery of the same query. Keep this distinction in mind; the specific error conditions and recovery timing are left for review.

Put mined spend-map conflict in a separate section. At this point, all we have is its name and the fact that it belongs to this round's Chainstate 2026.09 closeout materials. We cannot interpret the socket failure as a spend map conflict, nor infer that the data conflict will disappear just because a network request can be retried. The actual generated file and the version that generated it need to be checked next.

Tentative decision: continue handling the Unix socket issue by "retaining URL, and investigating whether to remove socketPath and retry"; keep the mined spend-map conflict separate and do not apply the former's conclusion to it.

