# History of UID and Database Batching

"compact UID lifecycle" and "atomic database batch" both came up in the wrap-up discussion, but there is not yet enough evidence to treat them as the same implementation change. We are keeping two workstreams for now: the first tracks the lifecycle of UID from creation through propagation to the point where it becomes invalid; the second checks whether a batch of database operations completes as a single unit. The two workstreams can be scheduled alongside each other for regression testing, but sharing a title is not a reason to combine ownership or conclusions.

Two ways of documenting this were considered at the time. One was to use the transaction list in the UI as the main entry point and connect the two requirements by following call names; the other was to use it only as a lookup starting point and trace each requirement back to the actual interface and sample results. We chose the second approach. The location pinned for verification is `frontend/src/app/bisq/bisq-transactions/bisq-transactions.component.ts`. It can show what is directly adjacent on the transaction-page side, but cannot by itself establish implementation ownership for the UID lifecycle or database batching.

The local evidence retained in the record is the declaration at the top of the file:

```ts
import { Component, OnInit, ChangeDetectionStrategy, ChangeDetectorRef, OnDestroy } from '@angular/core';
import { BisqTransaction, BisqOutput } from '../bisq.interfaces';
import { Observable, Subscription } from 'rxjs';
import { switchMap, map, tap } from 'rxjs/operators';
```

The interface examples should therefore first show which kind of transaction object the page receives, and whether UID on this path is just a display field, a query key, or a value passed further downstream. Until examples are available, do not read the names `BisqTransaction` or `BisqOutput` as defining the storage model for compact UID. On the database side, keep a separate record of batch boundaries, the state left behind on failure, and the results visible to the caller; a page refreshing normally cannot substitute for this assessment.

For this version, we will retain the page file as a shared navigation point and keep the evidence and decisions for the two requirements separate; we will not fill in the implementation approach, rollback behavior on failure, or final ownership from this file alone. If caller results become available later, update the two specific assessments: "whether it is the same batch" and "when UID becomes invalid."
